    "text": "A conducting circular loop made of a thin wire has area <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3.5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>&#160;</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> and resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> It is placed perpendicular to a time-dependent magnetic field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mi>t</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mfenced><mi>sin</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>50</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> The field is uniform in space. Then the net charge flowing through the loop during <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>ms</mi></math> is close to",
    "image": null
